| **Purpose of this note** I am writing this ahead of our meeting on 24 April to give you a clear picture of what I have done since joining, what I am now proposing, and what I need from that conversation. It is deliberately direct. I want you to come to the meeting already knowing the substance. |
| --- |

**1.  What I have done in the first two months**

I started with a listening exercise. I met individually with every Head of Area I could reach – Just under 30 curriculum areas in total. The purpose was straightforward: to understand where each area actually is with digital practice, what the real barriers are, and who in the team is already doing good things that nobody is naming or evidencing.

**What those conversations produced:**

- A digital RAG profile across all 34 areas. Five areas are rated 4 or 5 (Concern or Urgent) and have been prioritised for coaching. 4 I need to meet with as they were unavailable for the first wave of meetings. Twelve areas have identified and named a Digital Lead. This data sits in the HoA Tracker, which is updated after every contact.

- Direct coaching and CPD delivered across Health, Construction, Sixth Form, Engineering, Employment Services, and ESOL — through Teach-Meets, 1:1 coaching sessions, and Digital Health Checks. Each session is logged with evidence.

- Two practitioner documents completed to support my work going forward: The Digital Skills Framework and the Learning Without Barriers proposal. Both are consultation documents developed through the HoA, internal and external CPD, and Lois Knight conversations. 

| **What those two documents are — and what they are not** **Digital Skills Framework** maps each of the 34 curriculum areas to the DfE Essential Digital Skills categories and to the industry-specific tools employers in those sectors actually use. I open it in every HoA meeting as a conversation starter. It is a coaching tool, not a policy document. Heads of Area confirm, challenge, and add to it as conversations develop. The important point here is that the industry-specific tools and digital skills are to be refined with HoA in conversation, so that we have a current record of what Digital Skills are being taught, and how we measure the progression of learners in these spaces. **Learning Without Barriers** is my proposal for how the college addresses digital inclusion — specifically for ESOL, SEND, FIP, and learners facing device poverty or connectivity barriers. It is grounded in Universal Design for Learning, WCAG 2.2 AA, and the tools already available in Microsoft 365 at no additional cost. This proposal, if successfully implemented, will have a significant positive impact on the learning experiences of our more vulnerable learners. But more nuanced is the positive impact it will have on our whole learning community. |
| --- |

**2.  What this has led to — the Digital Leads Programme**

The consistent finding across every HoA conversation was the same: there is already good digital practice happening in most areas, but it is invisible, unevidenced, and unsupported. A Digital Lead in each area changes that. It creates a named point of contact, a peer resource for colleagues, and a direct link between the area and the Digital Pedagogy Coach.

The Digital Leads Training Plan 2025–27 sets out how this works. The key points:

- One named Digital Lead per curriculum area — a teaching or support staff member, not a management grade, supported throughout by the Digital Pedagogy Coach.

- A three-phase CPD pathway: Digital Foundations, Digital Inclusion, Digital Innovation. Structured, progressive, and mapped to the ETF Digital Teaching Professional Framework and DigCompEdu.

- Termly Digital Health Checks (3 a year), cross-college network meetings (6 a year), and a Teach-Meet programme through which Digital Leads share good practice with peers.

- Every activity generates evidence that feeds directly into QIPs, CQRPs, and the SAR digital dimension – this is not a parallel programme. It is already aligned within the Quality infrastructure.

**3.  The Digital Toolkit — what it is and where it sits**

The Digital Toolkit is a structured coaching and planning resource built around the Digital Learning Pyramid. It is the framework that sits behind CPD, 1:1 coaching, the Health Checks, and the HoA conversations.

| **FOUNDATION** **Digital Foundations** | Staff confidence with core tools, well-structured Teams environments, consistent and accessible digital resources. The baseline. Every area, regardless of starting point. |
| --- | --- |
| **INCLUSION** **Digital Inclusion** | Accessibility by design — WCAG 2.2 AA, assistive technology, EHCP digital targets, inclusive practice for SEND and ESOL learners. Directly evidenceable against the Ofsted Inclusion standalone grade from November 2025. |
| **INNOVATION** **Digital Innovation** | AI in teaching and learning, advanced formative assessment, Digital Leads sharing good practice, 21st Century Learning Design, sector engagement. Where we are heading, not where we start. |

The Toolkit is what allows a TLAM or HoA to have a purposeful conversation about digital development without needing to start from scratch each time. It is the shared language. It also means that when digital actions appear in a QIP, there is a consistent framework behind them that can be evidenced at CQRP.

**4.  Risks, costs, and what makes this strong**

| **What makes this strong** | **The risks to manage** |
| --- | --- |
| **Evidence-first, not evangelism.** Every action is logged, dated, and linked to a curriculum area. The data already exists. **Built on existing quality processes.** Nothing is parallel. HoA Tracker, QIPs, CQRPs, and the SAR are the evidence chain. **Low cost.** The Toolkit, the Digital Skills Framework, and Learning Without Barriers use tools already available – Microsoft 365, Jisc resources, and the college's own infrastructure. No new procurement needed at this stage. **Timely.** The Ofsted Inclusion standalone grade from November 2025 means digital inclusion is now inspectable. This programme generates the evidence needed — as a byproduct of doing the work, not as a separate compliance exercise. | **Pace and engagement.** Five areas have not yet engaged. Without TLAM support to facilitate introductions, some areas will remain disconnected. This is the single biggest practical risk. **Role boundary clarity.** The Digital Pedagogy Coach is a coaching role, not a strategy role and not IT. That distinction needs to be held clearly – particularly as Learning Without Barriers raises asks that require decisions from SLT and IT, not from me. **Copilot and AI policy.** Staff cannot confidently engage with AI in teaching until institutional policy is confirmed. This is blocking several coaching priorities and is urgent. |

| **On costs** The coaching programme, Digital Toolkit, Digital Skills Framework, and Digital Leads CPD require no additional budget at this stage. The Jisc Discovery Tool is free for Jisc subscribers, which Weston College is. Century Tech for learner Digital Skills tracking is an existing college resource, current not utilised. The principal cost is staff time for Digital Leads – and the ask on that is modest: network meetings, Health Checks, and CPD that are timetabled within existing commitments. Where costs do arise – as an example, External networking through BETT, Microsoft, Jisc, and external conferences, estimated 3 per year. I would like to take some of the Digital Leads to some of these conferences to help build collective intelligence on digital skills and learning within the sector. This will help shape direction, coaching, and colleague support. Partnerships with Good Things Foundation for learner device support, or assistive technology software for FIP – these are asks for IT and SLT to determine, not decisions within my remit. However, I can support in this area. |
| --- |

**5.  What I am asking for**

Three things.

- **Confirm the Digital Leads Programme as live. **[The Training Plan](https://weston-my.sharepoint.com/:w:/g/personal/graeme_wright_weston_ac_uk/IQAes1J3jJ6GQI54AIQqAvagASm6ktekHeojpRxAzutXlHQ?e=FRZsvr) is ready. The role description is ready. Having this programme confirmed means I, with support from TLAMs, can brief areas with authority, and Digital Leads can be identified and inducted before the academic year.

- **Note and endorse the 10 Professional Accountabilities as the framework for this pilot. **These are the named responsibilities against which the role will be evaluated at the July 2026, January 2027, and July 2027 governance reviews. I am not asking you to sign off every action today. I am asking you to confirm that these are the right accountabilities for the role, so that I am working to a framework you have seen and agreed.

**The 10 accountabilities (phases 1–3, January 2026 – July 2027):**

1· Map digital and subject-specific skills to the framework (with HoAs, TLAMs, and coaches) — delivering the Digital Skills Framework as a coaching tool, not a policy document 2· Teams environment audit with HoA and Digital Leads, with follow-up training 3· Clarify Digital Leads vs Digital Champions and engage both structures 4· Accessibility by design workshops (iTrent, termly, NSI)

5· Accessibility support videos for Digital Foundations on SharePoint 6· Learning Without Barriers AT roadmap with IT and SEND (for ESOL and FIP) 7· Assistive technology support videos 8· AI coaching and guidance for staff 9· Lead digital skills training (Jisc Discovery Tool, ETF pathways, Century for learners) 10· External networks and sector events

*Phases 1–3 span Term 3 2025/26 through to the end of 2026/27. The full accountabilities document and current RAG status (1 Green, 6 Amber, 3 Red as at April 2026) is available on request.*

- **Agree next steps on Learning Without Barriers and the Digital Skills Framework. **Both documents are consultation documents — they are ready for your read and for a discussion about what the college wants to take forward. I am not asking you to adopt them as policy today. I am asking for a clear steer on which elements to progress, what requires SLT input, and what stays with me.

| **The commitment I am making** Every HoA conversation, coaching session, Teach-Meet, and Health Check is logged in my Evidence Hub and mapped to various supporting frameworks. By the October 2026 review I will have a documented evidence portfolio against every one of the 10 formal accountabilities. The work is being done. What I need from this meeting is the institutional backing that allows me to do it at pace and scale. |
